Q: Error: Element type is invalid: expected a string (for built-in components) or a class/function (for composite components) but got: undefined. React A: <p>I had this error when I tried to get rid of warnings about <strong>PascalCase</strong>.</p>
<p>The warning:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Imported JSX component text must be in PascalCase or
SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE <em>react/jsx-pascal-case</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>I changed all components in my React Redux Form from Control.text, Control.select, Control.textarea to Control.Text, Control.Select, Control.TextArea and started getting this error.</p>
<p>I changed</p>
<pre><code>&lt;Control.Text /&gt;
&lt;Control.Select /&gt;
&lt;Control.TextArea /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>back to</p>
<pre><code>&lt;Control.text /&gt;
&lt;Control.select /&gt;
&lt;Control.textarea /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and the error was gone.</p>

Q: Global filters PrimeNG Angular2 A: <p>If you want filtering based on multiple inputs then you need to implement the filter logic yourself. On the value change event of any control you can call a function in the .ts file and within that function you can filter the data source of your table.
For example, I have a bunch of list boxes (one per column) that appear on a side panel to display an amazon style filtering of my data table. The code looks like this -</p>

<pre><code>&lt;p-accordion [multiple]="true" *ngFor="let col of filterColumns; let i=index" &gt;
            &lt;p-accordionTab header="{{col.display}}"  [selected]="showAllFilters"&gt;
                &lt;p-listbox  [style]="{'width':'180px'}" [options]="col.values" [(ngModel)]="this['Selected' + col.display]" multiple="multiple" checkbox="checkbox" filter="filter" (onChange) = "advancedFilterChange(this['Selected' + col.display],col.column,'in')"&gt;&lt;/p-listbox&gt;
            &lt;/p-accordionTab&gt;                         
        &lt;/p-accordion&gt;  
</code></pre>

<p>The code behind looks like this -</p>

<pre><code>    private advancedFilterChange(values:string[],col:string,matchMode:string):void{

      this.programTable.filter(values,col,matchMode);

 }
</code></pre>

<p>Where programTable is a reference to the primeng data table in your html which you can access by using viewchild</p>

<pre><code>@ViewChild('dtPrograms') programTable:DataTable;
</code></pre>

<p>Hope this helps...</p>
